; The questions about the allocation of one scarce input (§16). They sit beside
; `market.writ` because a claims file is read as a model's sibling by `writ query`
; and `writ compare` — but they are not *about* it. They are put to `planned.writ`
; unchanged, by name:
;
;     writ check planned.writ --claims market.claims
;
; which is the whole reason questions live apart from models. The two models
; share a library, a schema, an instance, a move set and every transition name;
; they differ in one guard. So this file is the experiment: the questions are
; held fixed, and the verdicts are what move.

; 1. CAN it come out right? Neither arrangement is incapable — this is not a
; question about competence. It holds in both, and `writ` prints the route.
(property need-can-be-met
  "the steel can end up built at a plant that needed it"
  (possible (and (is steel.state built) (is steel.at.need high))))

; 2. Can it come out WRONG? `never` asks whether any reachable situation has the
; steel built at a plant with no need of it. Under a costless request, one can:
; the monument asks, the allocator has nothing to weigh the request against, and
; the steel is spent. Under a backed bid the same three moves are written down
; and the first of them never becomes available.
(property no-waste
  "the steel is never built at a plant that does not need it"
  (never (and (is steel.state built) (is steel.at.need low))))

; 3. And after it has come out wrong, can it be put right? `live` asks whether a
; good outcome is still reachable from EVERY reachable situation. This is the
; one that matters, and the one the tool exists for: 2 says a bad outcome is
; permitted, 3 says it is terminal. Steel welded into a monument is not a delay
; to be corrected by a better plan next quarter; there is no next quarter for
; that steel.
(property need-always-still-meetable
  "from every situation the rules allow, the steel can still be built where it is needed"
  (live (and (is steel.state built) (is steel.at.need high))))

; THE LAW, and who can break it. `signal-honest` (in the library) demands that
; the plant holding the steel be one whose request matches its situation. Every
; move that writes `ask` or `at` can break it — nine of the twelve — and both
; models are handed the same ledger, because both models have the same nine
; moves under the same names. Acknowledging a move is not conceding that it does
; break the law: `can be broken by` is about what a move WRITES. Which model
; actually reaches a violation is the finding, and it is not the same answer
; twice.
(accept ask-clinic-high   signal-honest)
(accept ask-clinic-low    signal-honest)
(accept ask-tractor-high  signal-honest)
(accept ask-tractor-low   signal-honest)
(accept ask-monument-high signal-honest)
(accept ask-monument-low  signal-honest)
(accept allocate-clinic   signal-honest)
(accept allocate-tractor  signal-honest)
(accept allocate-monument signal-honest)

; Which plants actually need the steel? (§16.2) The answer is the same in both
; models, at state 0 and at every other, because `need` is wiring. The fact is
; in the world, sitting in the model, printable on demand — and in `planned.writ`
; there is no move whose guard can read it. That is the whole difficulty in one
; line of output: the problem is never that the information does not exist.
(query who-needs-it
  (where (p plant))
  (is p.need high))
